<div style="width: 100%; padding: 20px; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> International Communication</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Communicating Panama's Importance</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow complex instructions in unfamiliar tasks. Interactive: Collaborate in planning multi-step projects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow complex instructions in planning projects that aim to introduce Panama's culture and heritage to the world (e.g., "First, the traditional dance performers must be scheduled, then the costumes must be prepared, and finally, the cultural showcase must be presented to the international audience.")<br>Can collaborate and ask clarifying questions about task sequences in projects that aim to communicate Panama's national and global importance (e.g., "When should we complete the setup phase?" "It should be done before the morning of the event.")<br>Can interpret roles and tasks from recordings of international planning meetings related to promoting Panama's global relevance (e.g., "The recording mentions that Sara will oversee logistics for the cultural exhibit, while Ana and Ivan will manage the trade presentation on the Panama Canal")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by playing short audio snippets (10-15 seconds each) of diverse sounds related to Panama's global importance: a ship passing through the Canal, rainforest sounds, a financial news report, a cultural music clip. Students listen and orally brainstorm in pairs what each sound represents for Panama's global image. The teacher writes their ideas on the board (e.g., "trade," "biodiversity," "financial services," "customs," "vibrant culture").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces key vocabulary for the lesson visually (e.g., flashcards, images) and orally, focusing on pronunciation. Words like "global," "trade," "relevance," "hub," "transit," "impact," "recognize," "ensure," "proficient," and "strategic" are introduced. The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /t/ in "trade" and /g/ in "global." Sentences using passive voice ("Panama is recognized for its trade influence.") and modals of necessity ("We must ensure its global relevance.") are modeled, emphasizing stress on modal verbs like "must."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses Concept Checking Questions (CCQs) and examples to ensure understanding of new vocabulary and grammar.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If something has 'global relevance,' does it matter only in one country or around the world?" (Expected answer: Around the world.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If Panama 'is recognized' for its Canal, does Panama do the recognizing, or do others recognize Panama?" (Expected answer: Others recognize Panama.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "International Communication".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students will listen to an authentic audio recording (approx. 2-3 minutes) of an excerpt from an "international planning meeting" discussing a multi-step project to promote Panama's role as a "global hub" for "trade" and "biodiversity." The recording features different speakers assigning roles and outlining task sequences. The register is formal and professional, simulating an actual cross-cultural business dialogue.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interprets and analyzes information from various sources related to global issues and professional settings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions (First Listening):</b> Students listen for the main idea. "What is the primary goal of this meeting?" "What kind of project are they discussing?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Second Listening):</b> Students listen for specific details and mark statements as true or false based on the audio. E.g., "The cultural exhibit must be prepared before the trade presentation." or "Sara is responsible for the financial services aspect."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Third Listening):</b> Students match speaker names (from a provided list) with their assigned roles or tasks mentioned in the recording (e.g., "Ana: manages trade presentation," "Ivan: manages Canal logistics," "Maria: oversees cultural exhibit").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific grammatical structures (passive voice, modals of necessity, relative clauses) and target vocabulary (global, trade, relevance, hub, transit, impact, ensure, recognize, maintain, key, strategic, significant, professionals, companies, multinational) within the context of following complex instructions and interpreting roles.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – "Listen and Complete":</b> The teacher plays specific segments of the international planning meeting audio. Students are given a partial transcript with blanks. They listen carefully to fill in the missing words, focusing on passive voice constructions (e.g., "The presentation ____ (is scheduled) for next week.") and modals of necessity (e.g., "All materials ____ (must be) approved by Friday."). This activity reinforces vocabulary like "scheduled," "approved," "maintained."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – "Instruction Sequence Listening":</b> The teacher prepares short audio clips of complex instructions, each containing a relative clause or a modal of necessity. For example, "The data, which outlines Panama's role as a transit hub, must be analyzed." Students listen and verbally paraphrase the instruction, focusing on the core task and the condition. Pairs can listen and take turns rephrasing, then check each other. The teacher guides them to stress modal verbs and Wh-question words like "What makes Panama a key player?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check – "Clarifying the Plan":</b> In pairs, students receive a short audio recording (e.g., a manager giving initial project instructions). Each student listens to the recording independently and writes down key tasks and roles. Then, they compare their notes, asking clarifying questions using target language: "When should we complete the setup phase?" "It should be done before the morning of the event." "Who is responsible for the 'financial services' aspect?" "The recording mentions that Maria will oversee the cultural exhibit, which emphasizes Panama's 'vibrant' customs." This activity reinforces polite inquiry (pragmatic) and interpreting details.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "International Communication".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Planning Simulation. Students work in small groups (3-4) to simulate an "international communication team" tasked with planning a multi-step project to "introduce Panama's culture and heritage to the world."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays a new, slightly longer audio recording (3-4 minutes) of an initial project briefing from an "international director." This recording outlines a series of complex instructions and general goals (e.g., "First, the traditional dance performers must be scheduled, then the costumes must be prepared, and finally, the cultural showcase must be presented to the international audience.") and mentions different "areas" like "ecotourism" or "scientific research" that "must be emphasized."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students in their groups listen carefully, taking notes on the instructions, roles, and sequences mentioned. They then collaboratively discuss and create a detailed action plan, including assigning specific responsibilities and establishing deadlines, based *solely* on what they heard.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">During the discussion, students must actively use the target language: passive voice ("The logistics are managed by..."), modals of necessity ("We must ensure..."), relative clauses ("...the exhibit that highlights biodiversity..."), and the target vocabulary (professionals, companies, multinational, hub, transit, sector, area, scientist, ecotourist, canal, impact). They are encouraged to ask and answer clarifying questions to ensure mutual understanding, demonstrating respectful language and active listening.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group then orally presents their finalized action plan, explaining how they interpreted the instructions and distributed tasks.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment during group planning and presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Groups submit their written action plan (developed from the listening input) and present a summary of it. The teacher observes their collaborative discussion and their ability to interpret and follow complex instructions from the audio during the performance stage.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listening Comprehension:</b> Accuracy in interpreting instructions and roles from the audio recording (reflected in the action plan).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Appropriate and accurate integration of lesson vocabulary (e.g., 'global,' 'trade,' 'relevance,' 'hub,' 'transit,' 'impact,' 'ensure,' 'recognize,' 'maintain,' 'key,' 'strategic,' 'significant') in discussion and presentation.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Correct use of passive voice, modals of necessity, and relative clauses.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation & Stress:</b> Intelligible pronunciation, especially of /t/ in "trade" and /g/ in "global," and appropriate stress on Wh-question words and modal verbs.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration & Interaction:</b> Active participation, effective asking and answering of clarifying questions, and respectful communication within the group.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket with the following prompts:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of listening to the instructions and planning with your group today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List two new words or phrases you learned today that you can use to talk about Panama's global importance."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effectively did you clarify information from the audio recording today, and what specific questions did you ask?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What strategies did you use to help your group understand the multi-step instructions and agree on the project plan?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Today's lesson focused on developing listening skills for complex instructions and collaborative planning. The "Listen and Complete" activity proved effective for grammar and vocabulary accuracy. Students engaged well in clarifying questions during group work, demonstrating pragmatic competence. Some groups needed more scaffolding in organizing their notes from the audio. For the next lesson, I will ensure clearer visual aids accompany the initial listening input to support different learning styles and build on the vocabulary introduced. I will also pre-teach common phrases for asking for repetition or clarification.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson introduced key vocabulary (Panama, trade, relevance, global, hub, transit, impact, recognize, ensure, proficient, strategic) and grammatical structures (passive voice, modals, relative clauses) through listening. In Lesson 2, students will encounter a reading text that utilizes this exact vocabulary and grammar to further contextualize Panama's importance in international communication, allowing them to activate their newly acquired lexical and grammatical knowledge in a new skill domain.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>